00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:02.796
That's what makes this a true foundation for both humans and AI.

00:15:06.476 --> 00:15:11.636
And the tool that brings this data foundation
to life is Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio.

00:15:11.676 --> 00:15:15.276
Think of this as an AI powered, integrated developer experience.

00:15:15.916 --> 00:15:19.476
It provides serverless notebooks with built in AI assistants.

00:15:19.756 --> 00:15:23.516
It's integrated with SageMaker catalog for end to end data governance.

00:15:23.676 --> 00:15:26.236
On screen, what you see is a notebook interface.

00:15:26.476 --> 00:15:31.716
There's a Data Explorer on the left where you
can browse catalogs and discover data assets.

00:15:32.916 --> 00:15:39.156
In the main panel, you can work with SQL, create visualizations,
or even use natural language to query your data.

00:15:40.436 --> 00:15:43.156
There's also a data agent capability.

00:15:43.156 --> 00:15:47.596
You can ask questions about your data
in plain English and get answers.

00:15:49.036 --> 00:15:58.356
This dramatically lowers the barrier for non-technical team members
to explore usage patterns and derive insights for our use case.

00:15:58.356 --> 00:16:03.036
This is where we analyse search
query patterns, measure answer quality,

00:16:03.156 --> 00:16:10.076
identify gaps in our legal corpus and plan
product improvements all on governed high quality data.

00:16:14.436 --> 00:16:17.676
But building a great search system isn't a one and done effort.

00:16:18.036 --> 00:16:21.796
You need to continuously measure, analyze and iterate,

00:16:21.796 --> 00:16:27.116
and SageMaker Unified Studio gives us an AI
powered integrated environment to do just that.

00:16:28.356 --> 00:16:31.076
Here are the four key capabilities that we leveraged.

00:16:32.836 --> 00:16:39.716
First, governance and sharing. This is about data
publishing and subscription across the organization.

00:16:40.716 --> 00:16:45.076
The marketing account and CRM
account each have their own S3 data stores.

00:16:45.516 --> 00:16:51.436
These are for usage patterns on ide.com,
such as the areas of law and the intents of the usage.

00:16:54.196 --> 00:16:59.116
Glue data quality rules are applied
to those data sets to ensure that they can be trusted.

00:17:00.396 --> 00:17:04.796
That data then gets catalogued and published to the SageMaker catalog.

00:17:05.556 --> 00:17:12.076
Then, in our product account inside SageMaker studio,
we simply subscribe to those data assets.

00:17:12.156 --> 00:17:14.716
No data copying, no ad hoc access requests.

00:17:14.716 --> 00:17:18.436
It's a governed self-service model for cross team data sharing.

00:17:20.716 --> 00:17:23.796
The second capability that we leverage on is notebooks.

00:17:24.076 --> 00:17:28.716
We use Jupyter notebooks to process
and visualize usage data and run experiments.

00:17:28.716 --> 00:17:32.556
For example, how are users interacting with our search?

00:17:33.276 --> 00:17:35.836
Which queries return low relevance results?

00:17:36.436 --> 00:17:38.396
Where are the gaps in our coverage?

00:17:38.596 --> 00:17:44.396
Our data scientists can explore all of this with built
in AI assistants without managing any infrastructure.

00:17:46.596 --> 00:17:53.156
Third, the query editor. This gives us SQL
based exploration of structured data through Athena,

00:17:53.436 --> 00:17:57.036
or the built in query editor on SageMaker Unified Studio.

00:17:57.036 --> 00:18:04.716
We can query our entire query history, search patterns,
click through rates and relevant scores using familiar SQL.

00:18:05.316 --> 00:18:10.116
It's fast, it's serverless,
and it connects directly to our cataloged data.

00:18:12.156 --> 00:18:16.796
And fourth, the Bedrock chat agent
with a knowledge base for structured data.

00:18:18.116 --> 00:18:23.276
This is particularly powerful. It lets us run
natural language queries over our structured data.

00:18:23.716 --> 00:18:29.476
So instead of writing complex SQL and analysts
can simply ask what were the top ten queries

00:18:29.476 --> 00:18:32.996
with the lowest relevant scores last week and get an answer.

00:18:33.036 --> 00:18:34.196
Powered by Claude,

00:18:35.236 --> 00:18:42.996
the. The key takeaway here is the flow of raw data
and S3 quality checks with glue cataloging and publishing,

00:18:42.996 --> 00:18:45.716
then subscription and exploration in Unified Studio.

00:18:45.996 --> 00:18:48.156
It's a clean separation of concerns.

00:18:48.316 --> 00:18:53.876
Data producers publish data, consumers
subscribe and governance is built in at every step.

00:18:55.716 --> 00:18:57.876
This is what enables us to go from.

00:18:58.076 --> 00:19:00.716
We think search is working well to.

00:19:01.076 --> 00:19:05.956
We know search is working well and here's
exactly where we can improve next.

00:19:09.836 --> 00:19:13.956
Let me show you the practical workflow,
starting with how we query our data.

00:19:13.956 --> 00:19:20.796
The pipeline is straightforward. Data lands in S3. On screen you
see the query history and interactions info folders in S3 bucket.

00:19:21.596 --> 00:19:27.076
Technical metadata is registered in the glue Data
catalog with the field names and types recorded.

00:19:27.796 --> 00:19:33.036
We then apply glue data quality rules to assess
the completeness and characteristics of the data.

00:19:33.036 --> 00:19:38.836
Things like null rates, schema conformance,
and whether values in a column are part of those in a fixed list,

00:19:38.836 --> 00:19:41.796
such as areas of law which are being shown on screen now.

00:19:43.356 --> 00:19:49.276
And finally, we use Amazon Athena
to run interactive SQL queries directly against the data

00:19:49.356 --> 00:19:53.476
in S3. No data movement, no ETL into a separate warehouse.

00:19:53.476 --> 00:19:57.636
Just query the data in place and using the right SQL queries,

00:19:57.636 --> 00:20:00.000
we can answer questions like how many clicks did
it take to get the answer based on each intent?

